// lexdb over TDS: what the pipeline took from the row engine in Q8's R4b (design/lexdb-layers.md 6.3.Q8 R4b) through the
// T-SQL door, checked against SQL Server 2022 (run with LEXDB_MSSQL set to its connection string, this test prints SQL Server's
// answers: the expect lines): CROSS APPLY and OUTER APPLY after the first table (a dependent join): TOP and ORDER BY in it, no
// rows for a row (NULLs), NULL outer values, repeated outer values, varchar keys, an empty first table, an error in a later row
// in it and in an output (8134), TOP over it, two APPLYs (the second reading both earlier tables), an APPLY inside an APPLY,
// after a join, under an aggregate, a window and DISTINCT, per outer row in EXISTS, more rows than a batch, VALUES over the
// row. Against lexdb each batch runs with SET lexdb_batch = off (the row engine) and on: "same" and the answer, or DIFFERENT
// and both.
// expect: same: id,w: 1|37;1|370;2|74;2|407;3|111;3|444;4|148;4|481;5|185;5|518;6|222;6|555;7|333;7|666;8|37;8|370;9|74;9|407;10|111;10|444;11|148;11|481;12|185;12|518;13|222;13|555;14|333;14|666;15|37;15|370;16|74;16|407;17|111;17|444;18|148;18|481;19|185;19|518;20|222;20|555;21|333;21|666;22|37;22|370;23|74;23|407;24|111;24|444;25|148;25|481;26|185;26|518;27|222;27|555;28|333;28|666;29|37;29|370
